WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.460
Building a modern product requires maintaining multiple platforms,

2
00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:07.080
a web application, a mobile application,

3
00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.580
and the backend services that support them.

4
00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:14.940
Standard development workflows often isolate each platform into its own repository.

5
00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:18.900
This separation forces teams to duplicate API clients,

6
00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:22.740
TypeScript types, and core business logic for every code base.

7
00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:25.460
This isolation creates version drift.

8
00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:30.460
As shown here, the web application might be running on a newer version of the API schema,

9
00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:34.460
while the mobile app remains stuck on an older, incompatible version.

10
00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:38.460
Managing these disjointed repositories introduces friction.

11
00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:41.460
Developers must coordinate separate pull requests

12
00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:47.060
and manually sync changes, which slows down feature velocity across the entire product.

13
00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:53.060
Amano repo eliminates this friction by unifying all platforms into a single repository.

14
00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:57.760
This architecture establishes a single version of truth for every dependency.

15
00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:02.360
Native mobile developers already use a similar approach to manage complex projects.

16
00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:07.560
This mapping shows how an Android project uses separate Gradle modules for core logic,

17
00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:10.660
data, and UI components within one repo.

18
00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:14.660
The JavaScript workspace concept follows the exact same logic.

19
00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:20.960
Adopting this model involves implementing a specific dependency architecture across the entire workspace.

20
00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:25.160
The first step is physically separating executable code from shared logic.

21
00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:29.760
We organize the repo into two main directories, apps and packages.

22
00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:32.760
The apps directory holds the consumable endpoints.

23
00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:37.160
Here, your React Native app and your web app exist as sibling projects.

24
00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:40.060
The packages directory contains the shared logic.

25
00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:46.060
This is where the API client and design tokens live, isolated from the specific UI implementations.

26
00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:50.360
A root package.json file governs the entire workspace from the top level.

27
00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:54.360
This root file enforces a single version for third-party dependencies.

28
00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:58.960
If the project uses React version 18, it uses version 18 everywhere.

29
00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:01.860
This structure prevents the version drift we saw earlier.

30
00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:06.760
Every application in the repo is forced to stay in sync with the shared packages.

31
00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:12.460
While the code is now consolidated, we need an orchestration layer to manage the build process.

32
00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:18.360
In a massive interconnected code base, running standard build commands is prohibitively slow.

33
00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:23.560
Developers shouldn't have to wait for every package to rebuild every time they make a change.

34
00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:30.860
Turbo repo functions as a task orchestrator, managing execution pipelines alongside standard package managers.

35
00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:34.560
The Turbo.json file acts as the brain of the system.

36
00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:38.660
It maps out the dependency graph for every task in the repository.

37
00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:41.460
This configuration defines the task pipeline.

38
00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:46.860
It ensures that shared packages are built and ready before any application attempts to consume them.

39
00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:49.460
Turbo repo also uses a global cache.

40
00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:54.960
It remembers the results of previous tasks and skips rebuilding any package that hasn't changed.

41
00:02:54.960 --> 00:03:01.460
When work needs to be done, Turbo repo executes scripts in parallel across both the apps and packages directories.

42
00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:08.860
For the developer, this means a single terminal command can start the backend, web, and mobile environments at the same time.

43
00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:13.460
To see how this works in practice, let's look inside the React Native application.

44
00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:20.860
The mobile app imports shared logic using standard syntax, referencing the workspace package as if it were an external library.

45
00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:27.660
Even though it looks like a remote package, the tooling resolves the import locally to the packages folder within the mono repo.

46
00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:36.060
Turbo repo handles the macro level orchestration, but React Native has a specific micro constraint that requires us to make one final adjustment.

47
00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:39.760
The technical hurdle for mobile development is the Metro Bundler.

48
00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:45.360
Standard web bundlers like webpack or byte navigate directory trees to locate dependencies.

49
00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:47.760
Metro operates under different constraints.

50
00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:52.660
By default, Metro's resolution is strictly scoped to the mobile app's project folder.

51
00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:56.760
When the mobile app tries to import a shared package, Metro hits a boundary.

52
00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:01.860
It cannot see the packages directory because it sits one level up in the hierarchy.

53
00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:05.360
We resolve this by modifying the Metro configuration.

54
00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:08.460
We use a specific property called watch folders.

55
00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:15.060
Using watch folders forces Metro to expand its scope, allowing it to see the entire mono repo route.

56
00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:22.060
With this configuration, Metro can traverse the tree and bundle shared packages directly into the mobile application.

57
00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:26.060
By explicitly breaking Metro out of its local directory constraint,

58
00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:30.560
we integrate React Native into the modern JavaScript mono repo architecture.

59
00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:33.460
This is the unified architecture in motion.

60
00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:38.560
A single source of truth provides data and logic to every platform simultaneously.

61
00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:43.460
We have successfully decoupled business logic and types from platform-specific code.

62
00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:49.460
The system relies on turbo repo for orchestration and a modified Metro config for local resolution.

63
00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:53.460
This repository is now structurally prepared for large-scale development.

64
00:04:53.460 --> 00:05:00.460
With the physical structure resolved, the next challenge is managing the flow of data within this massive system.

65
00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:09.460
Next, we will cover state management patterns designed for large-scale applications with many features and independent engineering teams.

